/*! \page developer_container_page Container Templates

\ref developer_param_page    (Prev/Up)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_backend_page  (Next)
<BR>

PSCF makes heavy use of a use a set of container templates that are 
provided with the package.  These templates are broadly analogous 
to the container templates provided by the C++ standard library 
(namespace std), such as the std::vector template, but differ in 
some important details, as discussed below.

\section developer_container_array_util_sec Sequence containers (Util namespace)

Most one-dimensional array containers that store data in CPU memory 
(rather than GPU global memory) are subclasses of a class template 
Util::Array that is defined in the file src/util/Array.h. 
This class template has a template declaration signature
\code
   template <typename Data> class Array;
\endcode
in which the typename parameter Data is the data type of each element of 
an array. For example, an instance of the class Array<double> is thus an 
array of double precision numbers. The Array class template is a simple 
wrapper for a C array of contiguous memory. It allows access to individual 
elements via an overloaded subscript [] operator, using the same syntax 
for element access as that used by built-in C-arrays, or by std::vector, 
using indices that start from 0. If object A is an instance of class 
Array<double>, then A[2] represents a double precision floating point
variable that is element number 2 (the third element) in the underlying 
C array of double numbers.  The Array class template is designed to be 
used only as a base class, to define an interface for element access. 
Specializations of Array may not be directly constructed, and exist 
only within instances of subclasses.

The most common types of containers used in PSCF are specializations
of the class template Util::DArray. This template is defined in the file 
src/util/containers/DArray.h, and has a template declaration signature
\code
   template <type Data> class DArray
\endcode
in which Data is the type of each array element. Each specialization of
DArray\<Data\> is a subclass of Array\<Data\>, and inherits the subscript
operator defined by the Array template. An object of type DArray\<Data\> 
is a simple dynamically allocated array that wraps a contiguous block
of memory that is allocated on the heap. The block of memory that is
owned by a DArray can be allocated by invoking a member function named 
"allocate", which takes the desired number of elements as a parameter. 
This block of memory may be explicitly deleted by calling the deallocate 
member function, or will be automatically deleted when the parent DArray 
is destroyed, usually when the array object goes out of scope.

The src/util/containers directory contains files that define a variety 
of other types of arrays and other containers. Among these are: 

   - A fixed size array template named FArray for which the physical
     capacity must be specified as a compile time template argument
     (analogous to std::array).

   - An array template named GArray that can grow without bound by 
     appending elements to the end (analogous to std::vector).

   - A template named RingBuffer that acts like a circular array of
     finite capacity, in which addition of a new element overwrites
     the oldest element once the buffer has become full.

   - A templated named Matrix that represents a two-dimensional 
     array, or matrix, for which elements are accessed using a 
     pair of indices, but are stored in a contiguous memory block. 

Users may consult the \ref Container_Module "Container Templates" 
topic in the web manual and the source files in the src/util/containers 
directory for more information about these and other containers that
are defined in the Util namespace.

\see Container_Module

\section developer_container_array_pscf_sec Sequence containers (Pscf)

The Pscf namespace defines several other types of more specialized array 
containers.

The class template Pscf::FftwDArray is a variant of DArray that is 
designed to be used with the FFTW Fast Fourier transform library. 
This template uses a function provided by the FFTW library to allocate 
memory that is aligned so as to optimize performance of FFTW. It is
otherwise similar in most respects to DArray.

The FfftwDArray container is also designed to allow one container
to act as a shallow copy of a slice of another container of the
same type: An FftwDArray\<Data\> container may act as a "data view" 
(or shallow copy) of a slice of a data block that is owned by another 
FftwDArray\<Data\> container, known as the "data owner". When such 
an association is created between two such containers, a pointer
owned by the data view is assigned the address of the first element 
of a slice of memory owned by the data owner, and internal flags are 
set in both containers to indicate which container owns the data.  
Care is required when using this to make sure that all references
to the memory owned by a data owner are released before the memory
is deleted, to prevent the creation of dangling pointers.  As a 
safeguard, the FftwDArray class template uses a simple reference 
counting system, and halts execution in response to any attempt 
to delete memory that is still referred to by one or more other 
containers.

\section developer_container_rationale_sec Rationale for home-grown containers

The decision to rely primarily on DArray and other home-grown containers
rather than those provided by the C++ standard library was driven by two 
considerations. These were: 
 
   -  A need for a portable mechanism to optionally enforce run-time
      checking of all array bounds to facilitate debugging. 

   -  A preference for the type of interface for memory management that 
      is used by DArray and other array containers, which is different 
      from that used by any of the C++ standard library containers, 

Both considerations are discussed below.

<b> Bounds checking </b>:

The containers used by PSCF provide a mechanism to globally either 
enable run-time checking of array bounds, for safety, or disable these 
additional checks, to maximize performance. In PSCF, run-time checking
of array bounds during element access can be globally enabled or disabled 
for all indexed array containers at compile time by using the -d option 
of the setopts script to define or undefine a C/C++ preprocessor macro 
named UTIL_DEBUG.  This feature allows developers to globally enable 
bounds-checking during debugging and testing (e.g., to help track the 
origin of a segmentation fault), and then disable it in production code 
to maximize performance.  

The sequence containers in the C++ standard library, such as std::vector, 
do not check array bounds by default.  As a result, these containers are 
subject to segmentation faults caused by out-of-bounds element access, 
much like bare C arrays.  At the time of writing, the C++ language standard 
does not define a convenient, portable way to globally enable or disable 
bounds checking of sequence containers at compile time.  The perceived
need for optional bounds checking was the most important reason for the
decision to prefer home-grown sequence containers such as DArray over 
C++ standard library sequence containers such as std::vector in PSCF.

Associative containers such as std::map that are provided by the standard 
library, are, however, used in PSCF whenever an associative container is 
needed. 

<b> Memory management interface </b>:

The interface for memory management used by DArray and most other array
containers used by PSCF treats allocation as a one-time event that is
separate from both construction of a container object and from the 
addition of individual elements.  This is different from the interfaces 
used by either a C++ std::vector, which is a dynamic array that can grow
or be moved at any time by appending elements to the array, or by a 
std::array, which is fixed size array whose size must be known at compile 
time. 

The explicit allocate/deallocate interface used by DArray is similar to 
the interface used by the built-in arrays provided by Fortran 90/95, which 
were used in the older Fortran version of PSCF. This type of explicit 
allocate/deallocate interface appears to us to provide a natural fit for 
many scientific computing problems. Specifically, it is a natural fit
for problems in which the information needed to determine the size of 
the arrays is obtained from a parameter file that is read as one of the 
first stages of program execution, and does not change during later 
stages of execution.

